  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The most suitable method is to use <b>the AWS CLI command <code>aws iam generate-service-last-accessed-details</code></b> to gather last service access details for IAM entities such as users, groups, roles, or policies. This tool provides comprehensive and structured reports on when services were last accessed by these IAM resources, which can assist in the analysis and adjustment of permission policies across an AWS Organization.</p> <p>Let’s review why the other options are not correct:</p> <ul> <li><i>Utilize AWS Resource Access Manager to download comprehensive service access reports for any organizational unit, individual account, or the entire Organization.</i> – There is no feature within AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM) that allows you to generate service usage or last accessed reports for OUs or accounts.</li> <li><i>Log in using the root user of your Organization via the AWS Console, and view or export last access data for each delegated entity such as OUs or individual accounts from within AWS Organizations.</i> – While AWS Organizations provides some high-level information, it does not offer granular, downloadable last accessed reports at the account or OU level via the console for all resources within the entire Organization.</li> <li><i>Access the AWS Console or use the CLI to check the service access report found within IAM for Organization-wide activities, and review the report to determine when services were last used.</i> – The IAM service access report is limited to specific IAM resources and does not natively aggregate last accessed data at the Organization or OU level through the console for all AWS accounts.</li> </ul> <p><b>Tip:</b> For organization-wide optimization, use automation (like scripting AWS CLI calls) to systematically generate and review these access details across all resources in your Organization.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You are responsible for managing an AWS Organization made up of multiple organizational units (OUs) and many accounts. Your objective is to find out when various services were last accessed by specific OUs and accounts. This will help you optimize the security policies to grant only the necessary services. <br><br>What is the best approach for obtaining detailed service last accessed information for different OUs and accounts within your AWS Organization?</p>",
